  const CFAS_CARDS = [
    // T1
    ["t1", "Give the classic AICPA definition of accounting.", "The art of recording, classifying and summarizing, in a significant manner and in terms of money, transactions and events that are at least partly financial in character, and interpreting the results.", "Remember the verbs: record, classify, summarize, interpret."],
    ["t1", "What is the basic purpose of accounting?", "To provide quantitative information, primarily financial, about economic entities that is useful in making economic decisions.", ""],
    ["t1", "Name the three basic activities of accounting.", "Identifying (which events are accountable), measuring (assigning peso amounts), and communicating (recording, classifying, summarizing and interpreting through reports).", ""],
    ["t1", "Bookkeeping vs accounting: what’s the difference?", "Bookkeeping is the recording phase: mechanical and procedural. Accounting is broader: it includes bookkeeping plus system design, analysis and interpretation.", "Exam trap: bookkeeping is part of accounting, not the other way around."],
    ["t1", "Which law regulates accountancy in the Philippines?", "RA 9298, the Philippine Accountancy Act of 2004. It covers the practice of accountancy, the CPA licensure exam, registration, and the Professional Regulatory Board of Accountancy (BOA).", ""],
    ["t1", "What are the four sectors of accountancy practice under RA 9298?", "Public practice, commerce and industry, education/academe, and government.", "Mnemonic: P-C-E-G."],
    ["t1", "How is the Board of Accountancy composed?", "A chairman and six members appointed by the President of the Philippines, under the supervision of the Professional Regulation Commission. Each serves a three-year term.", ""],
    ["t1", "What do you need to pass the CPA Licensure Exam?", "A general weighted average of at least 75%, with no rating below 65% in any subject.", ""],
    ["t1", "Who sets financial reporting standards in the Philippines?", "The Financial Reporting Standards Council (FRSC), which replaced the Accounting Standards Council. It adopts IFRS as PFRS. The Philippine Interpretations Committee (PIC) issues interpretations.", "FRSC has 15 members: a chairman and 14 representatives."],
    ["t1", "When no PFRS specifically applies, what hierarchy does management follow?", "1) Requirements in PFRS dealing with similar and related issues; 2) the Conceptual Framework’s definitions, recognition criteria and measurement concepts. It may also consider pronouncements of other standard-setters with a similar framework, industry practice and literature, if they don’t conflict.", "This is the PAS 8 hierarchy."],
    ["t1", "Name the common branches of accounting.", "Financial accounting, management accounting, cost accounting, auditing, taxation, government accounting, accounting education, and accounting research.", ""],
    ["t1", "Which reporting framework applies by entity size under SEC rules?", "Large or publicly accountable (assets over ₱350M or liabilities over ₱250M): full PFRS. Medium (assets ₱100M–₱350M or liabilities ₱100M–₱250M): PFRS for SMEs. Small (₱3M–₱100M): PFRS for Small Entities. Micro (under ₱3M): income tax basis or another acceptable basis.", "Confirm the thresholds against your instructor’s notes."],
    // T2
    ["t2", "Is the Conceptual Framework a PFRS?", "No. It is not a standard, and nothing in it overrides any PFRS or any requirement in a PFRS.", "If the Framework and a standard conflict, the standard wins."],
    ["t2", "What are the three purposes of the Conceptual Framework?", "Help the Board develop standards based on consistent concepts; help preparers develop consistent policies when no standard applies or a choice is allowed; help all parties understand and interpret the standards.", ""],
    ["t2", "What is the objective of general purpose financial reporting?", "To provide financial information about the reporting entity that is useful to existing and potential investors, lenders and other creditors in making decisions about providing resources to the entity.", ""],
    ["t2", "Who are the primary users of general purpose financial reports?", "Existing and potential investors, lenders and other creditors.", "Management, regulators and the public may find reports useful but are not primary users."],
    ["t2", "What are the fundamental qualitative characteristics and their components?", "Relevance (predictive value, confirmatory value, materiality) and faithful representation (complete, neutral, free from error).", ""],
    ["t2", "Name the enhancing qualitative characteristics.", "Comparability, verifiability, timeliness and understandability.", "Mnemonic: CVTU."],
    ["t2", "When is information material?", "When omitting, misstating or obscuring it could reasonably be expected to influence decisions primary users make based on the financial reports. Materiality is an entity-specific aspect of relevance.", ""],
    ["t2", "What is prudence under the 2018 Framework?", "The exercise of caution when making judgments under uncertainty. It supports neutrality and does not allow overstating or understating assets, liabilities, income or expenses.", ""],
    ["t2", "What is the underlying assumption of financial statements?", "Going concern: the entity is assumed to continue operating for the foreseeable future, with no intention or need to liquidate or curtail operations.", ""],
    ["t2", "Define an asset (2018 Framework).", "A present economic resource controlled by the entity as a result of past events. An economic resource is a right that has the potential to produce economic benefits.", ""],
    ["t2", "Define a liability (2018 Framework).", "A present obligation of the entity to transfer an economic resource as a result of past events.", "Three criteria: obligation, transfer of an economic resource, present obligation from past events."],
    ["t2", "Define income and expenses.", "Income: increases in assets or decreases in liabilities that increase equity, other than contributions from holders of equity claims. Expenses: decreases in assets or increases in liabilities that decrease equity, other than distributions to holders of equity claims.", ""],
    ["t2", "What are the recognition criteria under the 2018 Framework?", "An item that meets the definition of an element is recognized if recognition provides relevant information and a faithful representation of it.", "The old “probable” and “reliably measured” tests are gone."],
    ["t2", "What measurement bases does the Framework describe?", "Historical cost and current value. Current value includes fair value, value in use (assets) or fulfilment value (liabilities), and current cost.", ""],
    ["t2", "Financial vs physical capital maintenance?", "Financial capital: profit is earned if net assets at the end exceed the beginning, in nominal pesos or constant purchasing power. Physical capital: profit is earned if productive capacity at the end exceeds the beginning; price changes are capital maintenance adjustments in equity, not profit.", ""],
    ["t2", "What is the cost constraint?", "Reporting financial information imposes costs, and those costs should be justified by the benefits of reporting it.", ""],
    // T3
    ["t3", "What does PFRS 18 replace, and when is it effective?", "It replaces PAS 1. It applies to annual periods beginning on or after 1 January 2027, with earlier application permitted, and is applied retrospectively.", "Your textbook’s PAS 1 chapter is still the base for most presentation rules."],
    ["t3", "What are the five categories in the statement of profit or loss under PFRS 18?", "Operating, investing, financing, income taxes, and discontinued operations.", ""],
    ["t3", "What new subtotals does PFRS 18 require?", "Operating profit or loss, and profit or loss before financing and income taxes, in addition to profit or loss.", ""],
    ["t3", "Which category is the default under PFRS 18?", "Operating. It is the residual category: income and expenses not classified in investing, financing, income taxes or discontinued operations.", ""],
    ["t3", "What goes in the investing category?", "Income and expenses from equity-method investments in associates and joint ventures, cash and cash equivalents, and other assets that generate a return individually and largely independently of the entity’s other resources (for an entity without a specified main business activity).", "Share in profit of an associate is no longer operating."],
    ["t3", "What goes in the financing category?", "Income and expenses from liabilities that only raise finance (such as interest on loans), plus interest expense and effects of interest-rate changes on other liabilities.", ""],
    ["t3", "What are management-defined performance measures (MPMs)?", "Subtotals of income and expenses used in public communications outside the financial statements to show management’s view of performance. They are disclosed in a single note, reconciled to the most comparable PFRS subtotal, with the tax and NCI effects.", ""],
    ["t3", "How may operating expenses be presented, and what extra note does PFRS 18 require?", "By nature, by function, or a mix. If any are presented by function, disclose in one note the amounts of depreciation, amortization, employee benefits, impairment losses and inventory write-downs.", ""],
    ["t3", "Into which two groups is OCI split? Give examples.", "Items that will not be reclassified to profit or loss (revaluation surplus, remeasurements of defined benefit plans, equity investments at FVOCI) and items that will be reclassified when conditions are met (debt investments at FVOCI, translation of a foreign operation, effective portion of cash flow hedges).", ""],
    ["t3", "What is a complete set of financial statements?", "Statement of financial position, statement(s) of financial performance (profit or loss and comprehensive income), statement of changes in equity, statement of cash flows, notes, and comparative information.", ""],
    ["t3", "When is a third statement of financial position required?", "When a policy is applied retrospectively, items are restated, or items are reclassified, and the change has a material effect on the statement at the beginning of the preceding period.", ""],
    ["t3", "What do PFRS 18’s aggregation and disaggregation principles say?", "Aggregate items with shared characteristics and disaggregate items with dissimilar ones. Use the label “other” only when no more informative label is available.", ""],
    // T4
    ["t4", "When is an asset classified as current?", "When it is expected to be realized, sold or consumed in the normal operating cycle; held primarily for trading; expected to be realized within 12 months after the reporting period; or is cash or a cash equivalent not restricted for at least 12 months.", ""],
    ["t4", "When is a liability classified as current?", "When it is expected to be settled in the normal operating cycle; held primarily for trading; due within 12 months after the reporting period; or the entity has no right at the end of the reporting period to defer settlement for at least 12 months.", ""],
    ["t4", "A loan due within 12 months is refinanced after the reporting date. Current or noncurrent?", "Current. Refinancing after the reporting period doesn’t change classification. It is noncurrent only if, at the end of the reporting period, the entity had the right to roll it over for at least 12 months under an existing facility.", "Classic exam trap: look at what existed at year-end."],
    ["t4", "A covenant breach makes a long-term loan payable on demand. Classification?", "Current, unless by the end of the reporting period the lender agreed to a grace period ending at least 12 months after the reporting date, during which it can’t demand repayment.", "Lender agreement after year-end doesn’t help."],
    ["t4", "Do covenants the entity must meet after the reporting date affect classification?", "No. Only covenants the entity must comply with on or before the reporting date affect the right to defer settlement. Future covenants are disclosed instead.", ""],
    ["t4", "What is the operating cycle?", "The time between acquiring assets for processing and their realization in cash. When it isn’t clearly identifiable, it is assumed to be 12 months.", ""],
    ["t4", "How are deferred tax assets and liabilities classified?", "Never as current, when the entity uses a current/noncurrent classification.", ""],
    ["t4", "What line item did PFRS 18 add to the statement of financial position?", "Goodwill, as a separate required line item.", ""],
    ["t4", "When is a liquidity-based presentation allowed instead of current/noncurrent?", "When it provides information that is reliable and more relevant, such as for financial institutions. Assets and liabilities are then presented in order of liquidity.", ""],
    ["t4", "May assets and liabilities be offset?", "No, unless a PFRS requires or permits it.", ""],
    // T5
    ["t5", "What are cash equivalents?", "Short-term, highly liquid investments readily convertible to known amounts of cash and subject to insignificant risk of changes in value, normally maturing within three months from the date of acquisition.", "Count the three months from acquisition, not from issue."],
    ["t5", "Are bank overdrafts part of cash and cash equivalents?", "Yes, when they are repayable on demand and form an integral part of the entity’s cash management.", ""],
    ["t5", "Name the three classes of cash flows and give an example of each.", "Operating (cash from customers, payments to suppliers and employees), investing (buying or selling PPE and investments), financing (issuing shares, borrowing, repaying loans).", ""],
    ["t5", "Under the PFRS 18 amendments to PAS 7, where do interest and dividends go?", "For entities without a specified main business activity: interest paid and dividends paid in financing; interest received and dividends received in investing. The old free choice is removed.", ""],
    ["t5", "How are income taxes paid classified?", "Operating, unless they can be specifically identified with investing or financing activities.", ""],
    ["t5", "What is the starting point of the indirect method after PFRS 18?", "Operating profit or loss (previously profit or loss).", ""],
    ["t5", "Indirect method: how do changes in working capital adjust profit?", "Add decreases in current operating assets and increases in current operating liabilities. Deduct increases in current operating assets and decreases in current operating liabilities. Add back noncash expenses like depreciation; deduct gains on sale of noncurrent assets.", ""],
    ["t5", "How are noncash investing and financing transactions reported?", "They are excluded from the statement of cash flows and disclosed elsewhere, for example acquiring an asset by issuing shares, or converting debt to equity.", ""],
    ["t5", "How do you compute cash collected from customers (direct method)?", "Sales minus the increase in accounts receivable (or plus the decrease), adjusted for write-offs where relevant.", "Cash paid to suppliers = purchases − increase in accounts payable; purchases = cost of sales + increase in inventory."],
    ["t5", "Which method does PAS 7 encourage for operating activities?", "The direct method, because it shows information useful for estimating future cash flows. The indirect method is still allowed.", ""],
    // T6
    ["t6", "How are changes in policy, changes in estimate, and prior-period errors applied?", "Change in policy: retrospectively (unless impracticable). Change in estimate: prospectively. Prior-period error: retrospective restatement.", "Mnemonic: Policy and Error look back; Estimates move forward."],
    ["t6", "Give examples of changes in accounting estimates.", "Useful life, residual value or depreciation method of PPE; allowance for doubtful accounts; inventory obsolescence; warranty obligations; fair value of financial assets.", "A change in depreciation method is a change in estimate, not policy."],
    ["t6", "It’s hard to tell a change in policy from a change in estimate. What now?", "Treat it as a change in accounting estimate.", ""],
    ["t6", "What is NOT a change in accounting policy?", "Applying a policy to transactions that differ in substance from those before, and applying a new policy to transactions that didn’t occur before or were immaterial.", ""],
    ["t6", "How is a material prior-period error corrected?", "Retrospectively, by restating comparatives for the prior periods presented, or if the error happened before the earliest period presented, restating the opening balances of assets, liabilities and equity (usually retained earnings).", ""],
    ["t6", "What did PFRS 18 rename PAS 8?", "“Basis of Preparation of Financial Statements”. Some general requirements moved there from PAS 1, such as going concern and fair presentation.", ""],
    ["t6", "PAS 10: adjusting vs non-adjusting events?", "Adjusting events give evidence of conditions that existed at the end of the reporting period (settling a court case, customer bankruptcy, discovering fraud or errors). Non-adjusting events reflect conditions that arose after (a fire destroying a plant, a decline in market value of investments, a major business combination).", ""],
    ["t6", "Dividends declared after the reporting period: liability?", "No. No obligation existed at the end of the reporting period. They are disclosed in the notes.", ""],
    ["t6", "Going concern deteriorates after the reporting period. What happens?", "The financial statements are not prepared on a going concern basis if management decides to liquidate or stop trading, or has no realistic alternative.", ""],
    ["t6", "Which date must the entity disclose under PAS 10?", "The date the financial statements were authorized for issue, and who gave that authorization.", ""],
    ["t6", "When is a person (or close family member) a related party of an entity?", "When the person has control, joint control or significant influence over the entity, or is a member of the key management personnel of the entity or its parent.", ""],
    ["t6", "Who are NOT automatically related parties under PAS 24?", "Two entities simply because they share a director; providers of finance, trade unions, utilities and government agencies in normal dealings; and a single customer or supplier with significant business volume.", ""],
    ["t6", "What key management personnel compensation must be disclosed?", "In total and by category: short-term employee benefits, post-employment benefits, other long-term benefits, termination benefits, and share-based payment.", ""],
    ["t6", "Must a parent–subsidiary relationship be disclosed with no transactions?", "Yes. Relationships between a parent and its subsidiaries are disclosed whether or not there were transactions.", ""],
    ["t6", "When is a provision recognized?", "When there is a present obligation (legal or constructive) from a past event, an outflow of resources is probable, and the amount can be reliably estimated.", ""],
    ["t6", "Provision vs contingent liability vs contingent asset: how are they reported?", "Probable outflow: recognize a provision. Possible outflow: disclose a contingent liability. Remote: no disclosure. Contingent asset: never recognized; disclosed when inflow is probable; recognized only when virtually certain (then it isn’t contingent).", ""],
    ["t6", "How is a provision measured?", "At the best estimate of the expenditure required to settle it. Large population: expected value. Single obligation: most likely outcome. Equally likely continuous range: the midpoint. Discount when the time value of money is material.", ""],
    ["t6", "Which costs belong in a restructuring provision?", "Only direct expenditures that are necessarily entailed and not associated with ongoing activities. Exclude retraining or relocating continuing staff, marketing, and investment in new systems and distribution networks.", "Needs a detailed formal plan and a valid expectation in those affected."],
    ["t6", "What is an onerous contract and how is it measured?", "A contract whose unavoidable costs exceed the expected benefits. The provision is the lower of the cost of fulfilling it and the penalty for exiting it.", ""],
    ["t6", "Can a provision be recognized for future operating losses?", "No. Future operating losses don’t meet the definition of a liability.", ""],
    ["t6", "How are reimbursements of a provision treated (e.g., insurance)?", "Recognized only when virtually certain, as a separate asset not exceeding the provision. In profit or loss, the expense may be shown net of the reimbursement.", ""]
  ];
